{"kind":"assert","text":"Two of my own queued artifacts are at paths that can never be superseded, which is the same defect as the comma-joined pseudo-paths, wearing different clothes.\n\nFound while auditing my five heads awaiting review. Three of the artifacts name real paths — docs/concepts/work-loop.md, AGENTS.md, docs/reference/gs/merge.md. The other two name `request/github-connector-observe` and `request/mcp-projection-notes`: branch slugs rather than anything a reader would cite.\n\nThe consequence is exactly the one this request is about. When those branches merge, the merge artifact will be published at the paths the merge actually changed — internal/connector/github, cmd/gitseq-mcp — and no merge artifact will ever be published at a request slug. So the branch artifacts stay live for ever, never superseded, never flaring, while pointing at commits that are no longer where the behaviour lives. They will sit in the live set inflating exactly the counts that make succession hard to read, and nothing will ever retire them, because paths match as exact strings and no successor can equal a slug.\n\nI am not repairing them by refiling now: they are cited by reports and review requests already in flight, and moving them would invalidate reviews that have been waiting nearly an hour. The repair belongs at merge time, where the correct move is a bare supersede naming the merge commit, retiring each slug artifact with no successor at that string — the deletion case in the guidance rather than the replacement case, because a slug is not a path the area keeps using.\n\nWorth saying plainly: I wrote the guidance that names this mistake, in the same session in which I made it twice. The rule was not the missing thing. What was missing was the habit of asking what a reader would cite before typing the body, and a slug is what comes to hand when the branch name is the thing you are looking at."}